<ol class="breathe-horizontal" start="1"> 


  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07640">arXiv:2611.07640</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07640">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2611.07640">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2611.07640">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Beyond Instruction Override: Benchmarking Indirect Prompt Injection with Task-Tampering Attacks
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+R">Rongting Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Bertran%2C+M">Martin Bertran</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Avula%2C+S">Sandeep Avula</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lee%2C+C">Chia-Jung Lee</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Murdock%2C+V">Vanessa Murdock</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.07640v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Indirect prompt-injection (IPI) benchmarks predominantly test planted instructions that redirect a tool-using <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> toward an attacker-chosen task. Yet attacker-controlled content can instead tamper with the&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.07640v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2611.07640v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.07640v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Indirect prompt-injection (IPI) benchmarks predominantly test planted instructions that redirect a tool-using <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> toward an attacker-chosen task. Yet attacker-controlled content can instead tamper with the <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> of the task already in progress. A buyer&#39;s dispute evidence can cite a false refund policy to inflate a refund without changing the task. We define IPI by <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> rather than instruction-like wording: planted content constitutes an attack when it attempts to influence a decision that its source is not <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorized</span> to determine. We introduce a benchmark that makes this boundary explicit: in each of 90 validated cases across three tool-use environments, the user request specifies the source that resolves the targeted decision. Every attack tampers with a decision required by the benign task rather than replacing that task. Each case has attacks with the same harmful <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> that target up to six parts of the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent&#39;s</span> decision making: its objective, decision rules, available actions, expected consequences, reading of observations, or beliefs about the current state. Without a defense, attacks on three of the five other targets (decision rules, available actions, and state) have higher mean success than objective attacks across five LLMs. A detection-and-removal defense reduces objective-targeting attacks to 2.4% success but leaves observation- and state-targeting attacks at 9.5% and 12.1%; this gap persists under detection-optimized rewrites. A tool-policy defense still allows 15.4% attack success, close to 17.0% without a defense, because these attacks generally retain expected tools and manipulate values obtained during execution. Evaluations whose attacks redirect the task or target only the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent&#39;s</span> objective can therefore overestimate the coverage of these defenses. Code and data are available at https://github.com/amazon-science/task-tampering-prompt-injection.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.07640v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2611.07640v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 5 November, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> November 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">34 pages, 6 figures. Code and data: https://github.com/amazon-science/task-tampering-prompt-injection</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03982">arXiv:2611.03982</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03982">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2611.03982">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2611.03982">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Computation and Language">cs.CL</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        SLATE: A Large-Scale Benchmark for Long-Horizon API Selection in E-commerce Tool Use
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wei%2C+R">Rongzhe Wei</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Shi%2C+G">Ge Shi</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Cheng%2C+M">Min Cheng</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Ghosh%2C+S">Sarthak Ghosh</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Gorde%2C+V">Vaibhav Gorde</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Akoglu%2C+L">Leman Akoglu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+P">Pan Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+N">Na Zhang</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.03982v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Enterprises increasingly want LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to automate standard operating procedures over their software stacks -- mapping each step of a procedure to the right call among thousands of internal APIs, without hand-&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.03982v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2611.03982v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.03982v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Enterprises increasingly want LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to automate standard operating procedures over their software stacks -- mapping each step of a procedure to the right call among thousands of internal APIs, without hand-<span class="search-hit mathjax">authored</span> step-to-API mappings. The core difficulty is API selection at scale: choosing and composing the correct tools over a long horizon, judged only by whether the overall task succeeds. Current tool-use benchmarks do not test this regime -- they expose only a handful of tools, evaluate single-step invocation, or grade multi-step behavior with subjective LLM-as-a-judge scores that penalize valid alternative trajectories. We introduce SLATE (Synthetic Large-scale API Toolkit for E-commerce), a large-scale benchmark that tests large-toolset, long-horizon API selection in a realistic enterprise domain. SLATE couples (i) queries decomposed into hierarchical, tool-grounded procedures over a ~1,000-tool e-commerce library, (ii) a deterministic, context-aware simulator that returns recorded outputs for matching calls and default responses otherwise, and (iii) an end-to-end task-success metric -- a task succeeds when it reaches every reference <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> -- that credits diverse-but-valid trajectories while remaining fully automatic and reproducible. Experiments with six open decision models and the language models they are built on, from 3B to 12B parameters, show that even the best model completes only 5.0% of the tasks, establishing SLATE as a challenging testbed for reliable enterprise tool use. We release SLATE, its simulator, and the evaluation harness at https://geshijoker.github.io/slate/
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.03982v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2611.03982v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 2 November, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> November 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">20 pages, 13 figures, 6 tables. Camera-ready version for the NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on Agentic AI Benchmarks and Applications for Enterprise Tasks (AABA4ET). Project page: https://geshijoker.github.io/slate/</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01265">arXiv:2611.01265</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01265">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2611.01265">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2611.01265">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Software Engineering">cs.SE</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Toward Bounding Coding-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Autonomy in Software Maintenance: A Pilot Study of Boundary Contracts
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Eun%2C+H">Heeyong Eun</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Chung%2C+S">Seungyeon Chung</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Park%2C+H">Hyejee Park</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lee%2C+Y">Youngeun Lee</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Hong%2C+S">Sukhyun Hong</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Jung%2C+H">Hyewon Jung</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.01265v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        As coding <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> gain greater autonomy to modify software repositories, relevant context alone does not define their operational&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.01265v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2611.01265v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2611.01265v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        As coding <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> gain greater autonomy to modify software repositories, relevant context alone does not define their operational <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span>. An artifact may be relevant to a maintenance task while being inappropriate to edit; conversely, another artifact may be required even when it is not explicitly mentioned in the request. We explore Boundary Contracts, a machine-readable abstraction that makes task-specific artifact roles (editable, inspect-only, required, and protected) explicit before repository modification begins.
  We conduct a pilot study on 40 maintenance tasks from a real enterprise repository, comparing the same Codex-based coding <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> with and without Boundary Contract information. The boundary-informed condition improves macro edit-selection F1 from 0.440 to 0.682, required-behavior coverage from 0.429 to 0.673, and the number of strictly acceptable patches from 9 of 40 to 18 of 40. To probe whether these gains are explained only by improved file localization, we additionally analyze 21 tasks in which both conditions modify at least one identical file. Differences remain in edit selectivity and patch <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span>, providing initial evidence that explicit artifact roles may influence maintenance behavior beyond localization alone.
  This pilot does not establish the general effectiveness of Boundary Contracts. Rather, it provides initial evidence for a broader research question: how should the operational boundaries of coding <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> be generated, validated, revised, and ultimately enforced as repository-level maintenance becomes increasingly autonomous?
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2611.01265v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2611.01265v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 31 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> November 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">Accepted at the 2nd Joint Workshop on AI for Software Engineering (JAWS 2), co-located with ASE 2026, Munich, Germany</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
