    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Benchmarking LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> for Behaviorally Adequate Test Generation from Software Requirements
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Qi%2C+B">Binhang Qi</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+Y">Yiming Liu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lin%2C+Y">Yun Lin</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhou%2C+Y">Yangjie Zhou</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Sun%2C+H">Hailong Sun</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Dong%2C+J+S">Jin Song Dong</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.36584v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        As large language model <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> increasingly compile natural-language requirements into repository-level code, requirement-to-test generation grows in importance to provide validation constraints (or harness) to guide code generation. Yet it remains unclear whether current&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.36584v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.36584v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.36584v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        As large language model <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> increasingly compile natural-language requirements into repository-level code, requirement-to-test generation grows in importance to provide validation constraints (or harness) to guide code generation. Yet it remains unclear whether current <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> can generate tests that validate the required behavior when the implementation of that behavior is withheld. Existing test-generation benchmarks mostly focus on white-box testing, whereas the requirement--test-related study usually requires evaluation on semantic alignment between the given documents and the generated tests.
  We introduce Req2Test-Bench, a benchmark containing 5,086 execution-validated instances recovered from developer-<span class="search-hit mathjax">authored</span> behavior-driven development (BDD) artifacts in 8 open-source projects, spanning 5 programming languages and 4 BDD frameworks. Each instance pairs a natural-language requirement in Gherkin with its developer-written Test Implementation. Given the requirement, target interfaces of the required behavior, an <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> must generate a complete, potentially multi-file Test Implementation. Evaluation of 8 <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>--model--reasoning configurations reveals a substantial gap between execution success and behavioral adequacy, which is measured strictly as kill-completeness: a kill-complete test kills every mutant that the ground-truth test kills. The best-performing configuration, Codex with DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, produces passing tests for 82.8% of instances, yet only 33.8% are behaviorally adequate. We find that (1) scaling up <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> yields sharply diminishing returns in behavioral adequacy relative to generation cost; (2) under-specified requirements cause the information gap, including ambiguous setup and implicit expected <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span>; and (3) requirement-grounded harnesses that steer <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> non-determinism offer a promising direction for improvement.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.36584v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.36584v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

