      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+S">Shusheng Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Tan%2C+W">Wenjun Tan</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.35150v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Long-horizon web search requires LLM-based <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to integrate information across web pages through extended interactions under task-specific constraints. Evaluating this capability is important not only for measuring final answer quality, but also for assessing whether an&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.35150v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.35150v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.35150v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Long-horizon web search requires LLM-based <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to integrate information across web pages through extended interactions under task-specific constraints. Evaluating this capability is important not only for measuring final answer quality, but also for assessing whether an <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> acquires, verifies, and uses evidence reliably over extended interactions. Existing evaluations, however, mainly rely on simulated trajectories or on post-hoc trajectories reconstructed from final answers, rather than on retained real interaction traces, which limits the fidelity of behavioral assessment and weakens support for fine-grained error localization. To address this gap, we introduce LONGSEARCHEVAL, a trajectory-aware <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> evaluation framework that combines a unified <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> execution protocol with source constrained <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> verification and retrospective failure-node localization. Stage 1 verifies answer groups against <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-cited webpages without human-<span class="search-hit mathjax">authored</span> gold answers; Stage 2 audits normalized rounds and links flagged failure nodes to incorrect answer groups. We evaluate 16 models from eight families on 100 tasks, yielding 1,600 retained runs and 6,545 automatically flagged failure nodes. Trajectory collection and evaluation required 1,037 and 318 hours of cumulative runtime, respectively. Human validation yields 94.50% agreement on supplied answer-group labels and 98.11% feature-correspondence precision for audited round-group links. Together, <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> scores and linked trajectory evidence support controlled model comparison and retrospective inspection of answer-related content.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.35150v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.35150v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

