    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Securing <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agentic</span> Commerce Through Behavior-Aware Fraud Detection and Cybersecurity for Trusted AI-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Payment <span class="search-hit mathjax">Authorization</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Ogunsusi%2C+K+L">Kayode L. Ogunsusi</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.27483v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        The growing use of autonomous artificial intelligence <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> in digital commerce creates new security concerns involving&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.27483v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.27483v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.27483v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        The growing use of autonomous artificial intelligence <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> in digital commerce creates new security concerns involving <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> impersonation, unauthorized payments, compromised <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span>, and misuse of delegated <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span>. This research introduces the Autonomous Commerce <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Guard System (ACAGS), a cybersecurity framework combining supervised machine learning, behavioral analytics, <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-specific <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span> controls, and explainable AI. The framework was evaluated using synthetic financial transaction data augmented with simulated <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> characteristics and five adversarial scenarios. XGBoost was selected following comparisons with alternative classifiers. Across ten experimental runs, behavioral features improved mean F1-score from 52.12% to 67.49%, with <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-specific features increasing performance to 69.46%. The framework achieved detection rates of 99.77% for abnormal <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> behavior, 99.73% for impersonation, and 100% for malicious delegation and <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span>-scope violations. Compromised-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> detection reached 72.27%. ACAGS also supported APPROVE, REVIEW, and REJECT <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span>, with SHAP explanations providing model interpretability. The results demonstrate the potential of combining behavioral intelligence and delegated <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span> controls to strengthen simulated <span class="search-hit mathjax">agentic</span>-commerce payment security, while highlighting the need for validation using real-world transactions.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.27483v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.27483v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

