    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Recoverability and Verifier Strength: Two Measured Preconditions for Unattended <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Self-Improvement
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+D">De Wang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Qi%2C+P">Peng Qi</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.27465v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> platforms are starting to improve their own <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span>: they read a deployed <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent&#39;s</span> execution traces, derive a change, have an automated verifier approve it, and release it with no human <span class="search-hit mathjax">author</span>. How much of this loop can safely run w&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.27465v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.27465v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.27465v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> platforms are starting to improve their own <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span>: they read a deployed <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent&#39;s</span> execution traces, derive a change, have an automated verifier approve it, and release it with no human <span class="search-hit mathjax">author</span>. How much of this loop can safely run without a human? We propose two quantities a platform can measure. Verifier strength $σ$ is how well the available verifier -- often an LLM judge -- agrees with a trusted reference verifier; it limits how much a step can improve the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>. Recoverability is how cheaply a bad change can be undone: whether undoing it needs a new verifier approval, and how much of the old behavior a rollback restores. A step should run unattended only if both are adequate. On our platform this rule allows prompt, tool and skill edits to run unattended and keeps weight updates under human sign-off.
  Our main contribution is these two definitions and how to measure them. We built a platform that applies the rule and tested it in two ways: on the platform&#39;s own approval gate, and in controlled experiments on the $τ^2$-bench <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> benchmark. On verifier strength, the platform&#39;s LLM judge sometimes gave the same score no matter what it was shown (9 of 37 settings) and reported no error, so a verifier can look healthy while approving anything; its strength must be measured, and two simple tests catch the failure. On recoverability, restoring a saved copy brought the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> back to its original behavior, but a regenerated undo did not, so only changes whose old version is kept can be reversed without a human; and while a change was deployed, it altered the <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> of 17 percent of tasks beyond normal run-to-run variation, which no undo repairs. Together these support the rule: a change can run unattended only when its old version is kept and its verifier&#39;s strength has been measured.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.27465v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.27465v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 19 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
