    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Designing an AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> That Does Not Tutor: Design Rules for Socratic Probing in Introductory Physics
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Cho%2C+B">Byungmoon Cho</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22949v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;and the two have come apart: a student can paste a physics question into a chatbot and submit a fluent, correct answer without understanding it. We built a conversational AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> for introductory mechanics that only probes. It is designed as an instrument that a correct answer alone should not pass. Its key measure is cave detection: identifying a correct&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22949v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.22949v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22949v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Instructors need to know whether a student understands a concept, not only whether the student can produce a correct answer, and the two have come apart: a student can paste a physics question into a chatbot and submit a fluent, correct answer without understanding it. We built a conversational AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> for introductory mechanics that only probes. It is designed as an instrument that a correct answer alone should not pass. Its key measure is cave detection: identifying a correct first idea that the student abandons when a single variable of the scenario is changed. A single right-or-wrong item cannot see this. The <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> asks a student to commit to a prediction about an everyday situation, explain it without physics vocabulary, and defend that explanation against a changed case built from the student&#39;s own words. It does not teach, hint, confirm, or correct, and it reports nothing until the end. Such an instrument works only if its replies give nothing away. LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> tend to leak evaluation: praise, word choice, reply length, and pacing reveal whether an answer was right, and a student who knows stops reasoning. We report design rules that suppress this leakage, derived from eight versions of the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> and more than 40 scripted simulation runs. The obvious fixes often made leakage worse: listing forbidden praise phrases increased praise, and a single worked example in the prompt spread into unrelated conversations. Each changed case had to alter exactly one variable; otherwise a changed answer could not be traced to a single idea. The evidence is simulation evidence only: no real students have used the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, and the <span class="search-hit mathjax">author</span> wrote both the personas and the scoring rules. This paper reports design findings, not learning <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span>.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22949v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.22949v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

