    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22899v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Payment systems authenticate users, devices, accounts, and cryptographic credentials, but a relying party, such as a counterparty, payment rail, marketplace, or autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> runtime, generally cannot obtain compact, independently verifiable evidence that the party presenting one exact transaction holds current organizational&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.22899v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Payment systems authenticate users, devices, accounts, and cryptographic credentials, but a relying party, such as a counterparty, payment rail, marketplace, or autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> runtime, generally cannot obtain compact, independently verifiable evidence that the party presenting one exact transaction holds current organizational <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> to execute it under the applicable evidence, policy, budget, and approval state. <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agentic</span> commerce makes this gap operationally urgent: a software <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> can produce a valid signature while acting outside its delegated purpose, after its sponsor&#39;s <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> has changed, or on transaction details that differ from those a human approved. We describe the ChainIT <span class="search-hit mathjax">Authority</span> Protocol, a subject-neutral and rail-neutral pre-execution <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> and evidence control plane. The protocol separates durable identity, a versioned organizational <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> record (the <span class="search-hit mathjax">Authority</span> Resolution Pactvera), sourced evidence assertions, deterministic reason-coded policy predicates, and organization-configured dispositions; binds each decision and human approval to a canonical transaction digest; reserves capacity atomically so that limits hold under concurrency; and <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorizes</span> commit only through a single-use, sender-constrained Execution <span class="search-hit mathjax">Authorization</span> Credential consumed at the rail adapter, with append-only lifecycle evidence sealed after the terminal <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span>. An <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Subject Profile adds the workload registration, attested key hierarchy, and sovereignty boundary required when the acting subject is an AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, without introducing a separate <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> concept. We specify the commit-time execution predicate and bounded-freshness status rules, give enforcement profiles for fiat (ACH, wire, card, instant payment) and stablecoin rails, and state residual risks and non-goals.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.22899v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

