    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Matoi: Selective Revalidation of Consequence Evidence for <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Authorization</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+Z">Zexun Wang</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.21394v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Matoi separates reusable consequence evidence from current execution <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> and durable occurrence history. Its finite query-aware contract covers branches, ordered collections and negative lookups. A consumer reuses only its own checked evidence whose observations remain valid; signed requests, live grants, quota and uncertain&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.21394v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.21394v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.21394v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Matoi separates reusable consequence evidence from current execution <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> and durable occurrence history. Its finite query-aware contract covers branches, ordered collections and negative lookups. A consumer reuses only its own checked evidence whose observations remain valid; signed requests, live grants, quota and uncertain <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span> remain separate obligations. Conditional correctness arguments distinguish locally serialized claims from nontransactional remote effects. Two studies strengthen cost attribution and domain evaluation. A 1,440-cell matched-wire study repeats 144 <span class="search-hit mathjax">authored</span> inputs. With compact input bytes identical, reuse reduces complete lifecycle cost by 3.49 percent in JS and 1.79 percent in Python. Combined representation/reuse reductions against full proof are 10.87 and 16.53 percent; five of twelve family intervals for the matched comparison cross one in each language. A prospectively frozen AgentDojo Banking mapping covers 5/16 user plans and 7/9 attack plans. Both governed strategies preserve five exact benign transfers and refuse 35 reused attack-plan/policy crosses. Pre-satisfied utility predicates are reported separately from completed work. These are original scripted synthetic tools, not autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> or a real bank. Earlier studies retain cheaper protected-cache alternatives, policy-compatible phishing, provider duplication and post-claim revocation. Separate JS/Python consumers establish finite-profile interoperability within the same project; durable recovery rechecks evidence and retains uncertain reservations. The results support a bounded, auditable reuse and execution contract, not a new general incremental algorithm, arbitrary-tool closure, atomic third-party commitment or production superiority.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.21394v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.21394v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

