    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Evolve Together, Fall into Place: How Social Scaffolding Shapes Self-Evolving Multi-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Systems
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Hwang%2C+S">Seojin Hwang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Choi%2C+Y">Youngmin Choi</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lee%2C+H">Hwanhee Lee</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.19799v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Recent work lets LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> evolve their own harness, up to the team itself. Yet in human groups, the same abilities achieve different&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.19799v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.19799v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.19799v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Recent work lets LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> evolve their own harness, up to the team itself. Yet in human groups, the same abilities achieve different <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span> under different social conditions. Borrowing the notion of scaffolding from developmental psychology, we investigate whether the social scaffolding of a self-evolving multi-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> system (MAS), namely its team composition and structure of interdependence (e.g., collaboration, conformity, or competition), shapes how the team evolves. We present AGORA (<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> Growing through Organization and Relational Arrangements), a framework that varies the two axes independently across three evolution mechanisms. Across web search, codebase understanding, and document QA, we find that (1) evolution alone rarely helps, while the right scaffolding surpasses a strong <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> evolved alone by up to 25 points; (2) it does so by moving <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> to the most capable member while the others step back; and (3) this member, having grown within the team, outperforms the same model alone. Designing a self-evolving MAS thus requires designing its social scaffolding, which shapes not only how its <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> learn, but who gets to lead.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.19799v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.19799v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 14 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
