    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Why Do <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Teams Fail Across Boundaries? A Failure Taxonomy for <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span>-to-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Systems with Private States
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Shekkizhar%2C+S">Sarath Shekkizhar</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Jwalapuram%2C+P">Prathyusha Jwalapuram</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Ke%2C+Z">Zixuan Ke</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Patil%2C+V">Vaidehi Patil</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+J">Jiayu Wang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Bansal%2C+S">Srijan Bansal</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Nguyen%2C+T+H">Trong Huy Nguyen</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Jiang%2C+D">Dingfeng Jiang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+D">Dongqi Liu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhao%2C+R">Ruochen Zhao</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Lu%2C+W">Wei Lu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Carenini%2C+G">Giuseppe Carenini</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Bansal%2C+M">Mohit Bansal</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Savarese%2C+S">Silvio Savarese</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Joty%2C+S">Shafiq Joty</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.19342v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> are increasingly interacting with other&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.19342v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.19342v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.19342v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> are increasingly interacting with other <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> that act for different principals (people or organizations on whose behalf they work), such as a buyer&#39;s personal <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> negotiating with an enterprise&#39;s seller <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>. In such <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-to-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> (A2A) scenarios, each <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> has its own environment and holds information and <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> that the other cannot see. Moreover, the interests of each <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> may be aligned (cooperative), partly aligned (mixed-motive), or opposed (competitive). Existing multi-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> system failure studies assume one orchestrator, shared workspace, or single principal. We ask how A2A systems fail when these assumptions are removed. We study more than 2,500 recorded runs of A2A systems built from different <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> harnesses and models, spanning 488 scenarios across eight families (legal, CRM, security, commerce, etc.). From these traces, we derive $A^2$-Failures, a taxonomy of 20 A2A failure modes in four categories: (i) discovery and delegation, (ii) grounding and record consistency, (iii) verification, and (iv) conduct and fidelity. Three findings hold across every harness, model, and scenario studied: (1) discovery and interaction are driven by the phrasing of the task rather than the availability of peer <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span>; (2) no <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> verifies a peer&#39;s factual claim on its own initiative, so an A2A system inherits the reliability of its least reliable participant unless the protocol or mechanism performs the check; and (3) the aggregate <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> score credits the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> completing the task while staying blind to the coordination, verification, and conduct failures beneath it, so A2A systems must be evaluated per <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, against each <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent&#39;s</span> own principal. We release the run logs behind the taxonomy at https://huggingface.co/datasets/a2a-salesforce/a2-failures.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.19342v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.19342v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 15 October, 2026; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 13 October, 2026;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
