    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15714v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Healthcare <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> must transform longitudinal clinical evidence into reliable actions through heterogeneous tools and stateful workflows, conditions under which model-centric systems may lose context, misuse tools, fail to verify consequential transitions, or cross&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15714v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.15714v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15714v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Healthcare <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> must transform longitudinal clinical evidence into reliable actions through heterogeneous tools and stateful workflows, conditions under which model-centric systems may lose context, misuse tools, fail to verify consequential transitions, or cross <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span> boundaries. Existing medical-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> benchmarks primarily emphasize terminal task <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span>, making it difficult to determine whether execution failures arise from the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> model, the runtime harness, or their interaction. We present the Medical Harness Protocol, an <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span>-preserving framework that independently evaluates seven healthcare-specific process dimensions: Care Action Fulfillment, Clinical Resource Competence, Patient-Evidence Alignment, Clinical Workflow Continuity, Clinical Auditability, Closed-Loop Verification, and Clinical Conduct Boundaries. A normalized intervention trace further tracks whether runtime support is available, activated, delivered, adopted by the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>, and propagated into subsequent behavior. We instantiate the protocol as MedHarnessBench, comprising 342 executable tasks across FHIR/EHR operations, healthcare-administration interfaces, and multimodal clinical tools. Across more than 16,000 controlled task executions, MedHarness yields broad improvements in terminal task success, intermediate subtask completion, and healthcare-specific process quality. We further evaluate MedHarness against multiple general-purpose harnesses and use controlled single-fault perturbations to verify the sensitivity and discrimination of the seven-dimensional evaluation. These results demonstrate that healthcare-specific runtime support can substantially improve both task completion and execution quality, while revealing failures that terminal <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span> alone cannot capture. Code and resources are available at https://github.com/Tomry1114/Awesome-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span>-Harness-for-Healthcare.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15714v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.15714v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

