    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span>-Canary-Mas: End-to-End Evaluation of Protocol Risk in Long-Horizon Multi-<span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span> Systems
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+X">Xiuming Liu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Gai%2C+L">Le Gai</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Chang%2C+Z">Zhiyuan Chang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Li%2C+P">Peiyang Li</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhu%2C+B">Bin Zhu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wang%2C+S">Shuo Wang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Feng%2C+C">Chao Feng</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15529v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span>-to-&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15529v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.15529v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15529v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span>-to-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> protocols allow LLM <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> to discover peers, exchange results, and coordinate tool use. A locally successful protocol attack, however, may be rejected by a later <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> or propagated into a consequential task decision. Measuring local exploitability alone leaves this difference unresolved and provides limited evidence about which participant caused the final <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span>.
  We present <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agent</span>-Canary-Mas, an end-to-end evaluation system that follows protocol risk through long-horizon multi-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> tasks. It covers five risk classes and 13 subclasses across A2A, ANP, and BCS, with 975 matched malicious--benign task pairs in five business domains. A shared OpenClaw framework runs autonomous, tool-using <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> under different underlying models while preserving the task and execution setup. Matched runs separate local protocol success from downstream consequences; retained evidence connects participant selection, message consumption, tool activity, and task-state changes. We further evaluate a protocol-context Guard that checks an object&#39;s <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> at consequential handoffs.
  Across three model conditions, local attack success is frequently at or above 90%, while consequence conversion ranges from 7.1% to 100.0%. Cross-<span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> evidence raises macro-averaged Audit-F1 from 29.7% to 95.5%. On Finance tasks, the Guard lowers average ASR from 86.1% to 53.8% and PTCR from 65.5% to 31.2%, with a measurable completion tradeoff. These results show that protocol risk depends on how <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> use exchanged objects throughout the task, and that evaluating this path supports more informative risk assessment, attribution, and defense.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15529v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.15529v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

