    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        KedgeFlow: A Programmable Control Plane for Enterprise AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=He%2C+J">Jun He</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Yu%2C+D">Deying Yu</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.14810v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Enterprise AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> can execute altered versions of approved actions, concurrently reuse the same permission, or retry operations whose responses were lost. These failures arise when approval and resource execution lack a shared contract. KedgeFlow specifies language-independent contracts for trusted controllers to&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.14810v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.14810v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.14810v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Enterprise AI <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> can execute altered versions of approved actions, concurrently reuse the same permission, or retry operations whose responses were lost. These failures arise when approval and resource execution lack a shared contract. KedgeFlow specifies language-independent contracts for trusted controllers to <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorize</span> state-changing operations and privileged gateways to enforce that <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span> independently of <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> reasoning. Inspired by OpenFlow, it connects policy checks supported by evidence, signed permission for an exact action, a shared allowance that can be consumed only once, target-side conditions for applying the change, and retained <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcome</span> records. Under trusted issuance of exact-action <span class="search-hit mathjax">authority</span>, two conditional properties establish at most one successful claim per allowance and link every governed dispatch to a matching grant, claim, and subsequent dispatch <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span>, even when the <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> is compromised. A reference implementation exposes this execution path to <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> systems through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). In the single-resource evaluation, 6,560 concurrent ledger claim attempts yielded exactly one successful claim for each of 82 allowances. Boundary tests rejected unauthorized and altered requests before consumption. A 32-call gateway race produced one committed mock effect, and a retry after a lost response produced no additional dispatch. Deliberately permissive policy nevertheless <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorized</span> an undesirable effect, exposing the limits of execution <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span>. Evaluation uses three members of the etcd coordinated key-value store on one host, synthetic authentication and <span class="search-hit mathjax">authorization</span> inputs, and a mock target that rejects stale requests. Distributed reservations, enforcement of every production access route, native crash recovery, exactly-once effects at real targets, and interoperability between independent implementations remain untested.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.14810v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.14810v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

