    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.14807v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Evaluating autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> requires examining whether they can translate goals into coherent action across extended interactions. In persistent environments, this requires coordinating interdependent actions under incomplete observations, accounting for delayed effects, and adapting as earlier decisions shape subsequent opportunities. We introduce ASIWorld&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.14807v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.14807v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.14807v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Evaluating autonomous <span class="search-hit mathjax">agents</span> requires examining whether they can translate goals into coherent action across extended interactions. In persistent environments, this requires coordinating interdependent actions under incomplete observations, accounting for delayed effects, and adapting as earlier decisions shape subsequent opportunities. We introduce ASIWorld, a survival sandbox that combines persistent world and character state with character-scoped observations and a programmable asynchronous interface to study sustained goal execution. <span class="search-hit mathjax">Agents</span> pursue interdependent production and exploration tasks through direct actions or <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span>-<span class="search-hit mathjax">authored</span> programs that continue running between model calls. The evaluation assesses task success, the attainment of development achievements, and survival costs, complemented by an analysis of execution behavior. In an exploratory study of nine <span class="search-hit mathjax">agent</span> configurations using a common harness, eight complete the production task and six complete both tasks. Analyses of execution trajectories and performance across difficulty levels reveal different preparation, combat, and recovery behaviors among configurations with similar task <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span>. Incomplete preparation is associated with stalled progression, while configurations that complete the tasks differ in survival costs and the fulfillment of additional achievement criteria. These findings illustrate the value of assessing sustained goal execution through both task <span class="search-hit mathjax">outcomes</span> and the behaviors and costs associated with achieving them.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.14807v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.14807v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

